1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,910
Dividends coming in feel reassuring,
so many people collect dividend ETFs.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:09,000
But even after the tax withheld on every
payout, is more left than in an index ETF?

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:16,000
Let's backtest SCHD, a US dividend
stock ETF, against SPY, an S&P 500 ETF.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,440
From 2011, we buy $1,000 on the
first trading day of every month.

5
00:00:20,890 --> 00:00:25,860
Dividends are reinvested after
15% tax, and the fee is 0.25%.

6
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:28,450
Now let's run the dates forward.

7
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,700
The purple line is the SCHD
account; the blue line is SPY's.

8
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:35,630
Yellow dotted line: money put in so far.

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:39,160
Above: each account's dividends
received and tax paid.

10
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:43,290
$1,000 buys as many shares as
it can, fractions included.

11
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:49,100
Until 2017, the lead changed 43
times as the two traded places.

12
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:52,670
From January 2, 2018, SPY was ahead.

13
00:00:53,120 --> 00:01:00,540
In 2019, SCHD paid 63.1% more dividends
than SPY, but SPY's account was ahead.

14
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:05,190
In the COVID crash, both accounts
fell together until March 23.

15
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:13,880
The SCHD account fell 32.8%,
SPY's 33.4%: similar losses.

16
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,200
In 2022, a long bear market arrived.

17
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:20,130
In April, SCHD pulled ahead again.

18
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:27,620
That year SCHD fell 14.6% and
SPY 22.2%, so SCHD fell less.

19
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:33,100
On January 6, 2023, SCHD was
ahead by as much as about $33,000.

20
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:37,380
But on April 26, 2023,
SPY took the lead again.

21
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:44,150
In 2025 SCHD's dividends were still
2.6× SPY's, but the account gap widened.

22
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:47,870
October 15, 2026: the results.

23
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:51,500
Each account put in
$180,000 in 180 deposits.

24
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:57,550
The SCHD account had about $480,000;
the SPY account, about $590,000.

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,710
The SPY account had 1.22× as
much: about $110,000 more.

26
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:07,840
After April 26, 2023, SCHD
never got ahead again.

27
00:02:08,290 --> 00:02:12,670
SCHD was ahead on only
20.1% of all trading days.

28
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:18,640
SCHD received about $87,000
in dividends, 2.1× SPY's.

29
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:23,900
So it also paid about
$13,000 in tax, 2.1× SPY's.

30
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:29,640
Dividends over the past 1 year:
SCHD about $15,000, 2.5× SPY's.

31
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:35,030
Every year from 2012, the SCHD
account received more dividends.

32
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:38,910
It got more dividends,
so why was less left?

33
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:41,590
Let's see where dividends come from.

34
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,280
On a dividend day, the price
usually drops by the dividend.

35
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:49,470
Company cash goes to shareholders, so
the company is worth that much less.

36
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:53,630
So receiving a dividend doesn't
grow your total account value.

37
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,660
But the tax is taken the
moment you receive it.

38
00:02:57,110 --> 00:02:58,880
Let's look at a real account.

39
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:05,090
On December 10, 2025, 1 SCHD
share paid a $0.278 dividend.

40
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:08,400
For the whole account, that was $3,743.

41
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:14,300
15% tax, $561, was
withheld, leaving $3,181.

42
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:19,900
This money, minus the fee, was
reinvested: 115.1 shares at $27.57.

43
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:23,470
The index ETF pays
dividends too, just less.

44
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:30,000
2025 dividend yield: SCHD 3.8%, SPY 1.2%.

45
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:34,040
The more dividends you get, the
more tax you pay, and sooner.

46
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,470
Without monthly deposit timing,
how did the funds themselves do?

47
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:43,750
We compared each year's 1-year return,
dividends reinvested, without tax or fees.

48
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:49,880
In 4 of the 14 years from 2012
to 2025, SCHD returned more.

49
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:56,410
In 2022: SCHD down 3.2%, SPY down 18.2%.

50
00:03:56,860 --> 00:04:02,910
In 2023: SCHD up 4.6%, SPY up 26.2%.

51
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:09,920
In 2024: SCHD up 11.7%, SPY up 24.9%.

52
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:16,450
In 2025: SCHD up 4.3%, SPY up 17.7%.

53
00:04:17,450 --> 00:04:19,120
Did the tax cause the gap?

54
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:24,160
15% is the rate withheld on US dividends
for investors in many countries.

55
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:30,980
With 0% tax: SCHD about
$510,000, SPY about $600,000.

56
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:34,560
Even with no tax, SPY had 1.19× as much.

57
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:38,570
At 30% withheld, the gap grew to 1.25×.

58
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,160
What if you sell everything at the
end and pay 15% tax on the gains?

59
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:50,170
SCHD about $450,000, SPY about
$530,000: the gap shrank to 1.19×.

60
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:53,760
That's because SCHD had already
paid tax through its dividends.

61
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:57,910
And instead of monthly, $100,000
all at once at the start?

62
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:05,400
SCHD about $610,000, SPY about
$790,000: SPY had 1.29× as much.

63
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:09,840
The November 2011 start
may have skewed the result.

64
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:12,440
What if it had started in another month?

65
00:05:12,890 --> 00:05:15,028
We made 5-year monthly accounts,

66
00:05:15,028 --> 00:05:18,970
one starting each month from
November 2011 to October 2021.

67
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:20,860
120 in all.

68
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:25,400
SCHD ended with more
in only 25 of the 120.

69
00:05:25,850 --> 00:05:31,730
SCHD's biggest lead: the account
started January 2018, at 1.15×.

70
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:36,890
Conversely, in the account started
November 2020, SPY had 1.34×.

71
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:41,820
In the 10-year accounts,
SCHD was ahead in 12 of 60.

72
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:47,460
SCHD launched in 2011, so it
missed the 2008 financial crisis.

73
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:50,161
If dividend stocks are
strong in downturns,

74
00:05:50,161 --> 00:05:51,940
a span with the crisis may differ.

75
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:57,030
So we also tested VYM, a US
high-dividend ETF launched in 2006.

76
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:01,160
From December 2006, $1,000
a month, same rules.

77
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:09,720
In the crisis, VYM fell 29.2%
and SPY 33.7%, so VYM fell less.

78
00:06:10,170 --> 00:06:16,330
Until late October 2011, VYM was
ahead of SPY, at about $67,000.

79
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:21,820
Money put in by October
15, 2026: $239,000.

80
00:06:22,270 --> 00:06:28,430
VYM about $810,000, SPY about
$1,100,000: SPY had 1.35× as much.

81
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:33,750
Among the 10-year accounts, VYM
ended with more in only 5 of 119.

82
00:06:34,750 --> 00:06:35,870
To sum up.

83
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:39,520
We saved monthly for 15
years, with 15% tax withheld.

84
00:06:39,970 --> 00:06:43,800
The SPY account was
1.22× the SCHD account.

85
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:46,330
Even with no tax, it was 1.19×.

86
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:51,310
SCHD got 2.1× the dividends, and
paid correspondingly more tax.

87
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:55,960
In bear markets like 2022, SCHD fell less.

88
00:06:56,410 --> 00:07:02,260
Other start months gave some accounts
where SCHD led, but just 25 of 120.

89
00:07:02,710 --> 00:07:09,360
VYM, tested from 2006, also trailed SPY
in 114 of 119 accounts held 10 years.

90
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:13,670
This was a period when US
large-cap growth stocks soared.

91
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:17,360
In a period when dividend stocks
were stronger, results could differ.

92
00:07:17,810 --> 00:07:21,320
Taking dividends out for living costs
wasn't part of this calculation.

93
00:07:21,770 --> 00:07:23,952
Tax rates vary by country and account;

94
00:07:23,952 --> 00:07:27,570
health-insurance costs, currency
and inflation aren't included.

95
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:33,300
And the SPY side had to endure
years like 2022's 22.2% drop.

96
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:37,040
This isn't investment advice;
it's rules checked with code.

97
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:41,810
Got a hypothesis to backtest
next? Tell us in the comments.

98
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:43,810
Thanks for watching.
